DATA.lessons.push({id:"lesson-2",num:2,title:"Everything Is a Widget",description:"The core mental model: everything is a widget. Build your first StatelessWidget.",markdown:"# Lesson 2: Everything Is a Widget\n\n## The one idea that matters\n\nIn Flutter, **everything is a widget**. Text is a widget. A button is a widget. Padding, alignment, rows, columns — widgets. Even your whole app is a widget.\n\nA widget is just a **description of what the UI should look like**. You don't create buttons and move pixels around; you describe the UI you want, and Flutter figures out how to draw it. When something needs to change, you describe the new UI, and Flutter updates the screen.\n\nWidgets form a **tree**: big widgets contain smaller widgets, which contain even smaller ones. That nesting you saw in Lesson 1 *is* the widget tree.\n\n## StatelessWidget\n\nThe simplest kind of widget is a `StatelessWidget` — a widget whose appearance **never changes** once it's built. A label, an icon, a static title: all stateless.\n\nEvery stateless widget overrides one method: **`build()`**. It takes a `BuildContext` and returns the widget tree for this widget:\n\n```dart\nclass Greeting extends StatelessWidget {\n  final String name;\n\n  const Greeting({super.key, required this.name});\n\n  @override\n  Widget build(BuildContext context) {\n    return Text(\n      'Hello, $name!',\n      style: const TextStyle(fontSize: 24),\n    );\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNotice three things: the data comes in through the **constructor** (`name`), the widget is **immutable** (`final`), and `build()` simply **returns** what should appear. No step-by-step drawing instructions anywhere.\n\nYou'd use it like any other widget: `Greeting(name: 'Aarav')`.\n\n## Widgets you will use every day\n\n- **`Text`** — displays a string, with optional styling.\n- **`Icon`** — a Material icon, e.g. `Icon(Icons.star)`.\n- **`Image`** — shows an image from assets or the network.\n- **`ElevatedButton`** — a raised button with an `onPressed` callback.\n\n```dart\nElevatedButton(\n  onPressed: () {\n    print('Button tapped!');\n  },\n  child: const Text('Tap me'),\n)\n```\n\n## Stateless vs Stateful — a preview\n\n`StatelessWidget` can't change after it's built. But apps need counters, forms, toggles — things that change. That's what `StatefulWidget` is for, and it's the entire subject of Lesson 4.\n\n## Key terms\n\n- **Widget tree** — the nested structure of widgets that describes your UI.\n- **build()** — the method that returns a widget's UI description.\n- **Immutable** — once created, a stateless widget's properties can't change.\n",pdf:"data:application/octet-stream;base64,",quizId:"quiz-2"});